<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Pylos</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.) · capital Pylos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mycenaean kingdom of Messenia whose burnt palace preserved more than a thousand Linear B tablets, the fullest record of a Bronze Age Greek state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pylos grew on the Englianos ridge in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/messene">Messenia</a>, where rich shaft and tholos tombs show an emerging elite by c. 1650 BCE, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griffin-warrior-tomb">Griffin Warrior</a> burial found in 2015. By the 14th and 13th centuries BCE its rulers had unified Messenia into a single kingdom divided into two provinces.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The palace, linked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a>, was headed by a wanax with a lawagetas below him. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> archive records taxation of some 16 districts, landholding, bronze-smiths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and perfumed oil production, rowers for the fleet and offerings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potnia">Potnia</a>. Frescoes and a well-preserved throne room survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The palace was destroyed by fire c. 1180 BCE, apparently while preparing coastal defences, and the region largely depopulated. Its tablets, baked by the fire, were found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> in 1939 and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a> show in 1952 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> was Greek.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-pylos.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Pylos" width="320" height="239"><figcaption>Image: Alun Salt · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Bath_in_Pylos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pylos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pylos, Palace of Nestor</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1415&amp;focus=kingdom-of-pylos" aria-label="Kingdom of Pylos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5224 844 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Pylos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5544 1100h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1415&amp;focus=kingdom-of-pylos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

